      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $5{p}^{2}=3p$ are $p=${{field-1}} and $p=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{p}^{2} & =3p \\\\ 5{p}^{2}-3p & =0 \\\\ p(5p-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\np=0\\,\\text{or}\\,5p-3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\np=0\\,\\text{or}\\,p=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=0$ and $p=\\frac{3}{5}.$",
